**Instantiating a class.** A class is a blueprint; an object is an instance. In Java, `Student s = new Student("Asha");` does three things: allocates memory on the heap, runs the constructor to initialize fields, and returns a reference stored in `s`. In C++ you can create on the stack (`Student s("Asha");`) or the heap (`Student* p = new Student("Asha");`, which must be freed with `delete`). Other ways: factory methods, `clone`, reflection, deserialization.

**Compile-time polymorphism (static binding).** The compiler decides which method to call from the signature. Achieved by **method overloading** (same name, different parameter lists) and, in C++, operator overloading and templates.

```java
class Calc {
    int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
}
// new Calc().add(2, 3)     -> int version, chosen at compile time
// new Calc().add(2.5, 3.5) -> double version
```

**Runtime polymorphism (dynamic binding).** The method to execute is decided at runtime from the *actual object type*. Achieved by **method overriding** with inheritance and a base-class reference.

```java
class Animal { void sound() { System.out.println("..."); } }
class Dog extends Animal { @Override void sound() { System.out.println("Bark"); } }
class Cat extends Animal { @Override void sound() { System.out.println("Meow"); } }

Animal a = new Dog();
a.sound();   // prints Bark, since the JVM looks up Dog's method via the virtual method table
```

**Key differences.** Overloading needs different parameters and may differ in return type; overriding needs the same signature and an inheritance relationship. Overloading is resolved by the compiler, overriding by the JVM at runtime. In C++, runtime polymorphism requires a `virtual` function called through a pointer or reference. Static, private and final methods in Java are not dynamically dispatched, and fields are never polymorphic.
